40,2,1,talk,Kenji,,"Oh man, this is a bit scary! I found out our yard scanners are super old, and the 2021 trial was a total mess because people just went back to using spreadsheets! We really need something that actually works!","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,1,talk,Mizuki,,"My findings are equally concerning. It appears that while initial capital requests are often trimmed, recurring operating costs are approved without scrutiny. Furthermore, separately licensed add-ons have climbed yearly, creating a significant hidden financial burden.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,1,talk,Asuka,,"The data confirms my suspicions. Last winter's outage remained unowned for two days because support was scattered across four suppliers. Additionally, warehouse rules were rewritten twice mid-year, proving that our initial project scopes are fundamentally unstable.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,1,talk,Rin,,I found some really weird stuff! The dispatch tool is like a sleepy dinosaur—it's so old and they skipped upgrades because they were too busy! And the 2018 pay move was a total mess because the outside people left mistakes everywhere!,"{""words"":41,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,1,talk,Servas,,"My findings remind us that while systems are built of code, they are sustained by people. We are losing the wisdom of our administrators, and our crews often find official training incompatible with their lived reality.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,2,talk,Kenji,,"@Servas, that's so true! If the training doesn't match the real work, no wonder people use spreadsheets. And with our depots running 24/7 in winter, we can't afford any mistakes or ""paper dispatch"" days!","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,2,talk,Mizuki,,"@Kenji, you are right to be concerned about the operational risk. However, we must also consider that a system that fails the users often leads to expensive, unplanned ""fixes"" and add-ons that drain our budget over time.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Mizuki, exactly. The financial drain is a symptom of the underlying instability. If we cannot define a scope that survives a year or identify a single point of accountability during a crash, we are simply paying for chaos.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,2,talk,Rin,,"@Asuka, that's exactly it! It's like when someone ""fixes"" a toy but leaves a piece missing, and then you have to fix it again! We can't have a new system that just gets sleepy or comes back broken!","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,2,talk,Servas,,"It is a delicate balance we seek. We must find a solution that does not merely offer a list of features, but one that respects the limited strength of our people and the wisdom of the depot floor.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,3,talk,Kenji,,"I totally agree! We need a system that's tough enough for the yards and doesn't crash during the winter rush. If it's too fancy or complicated, the guys will just go back to their spreadsheets again!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,3,talk,Mizuki,,"@Kenji, simplicity is a virtue, but we must ensure that ""simple"" doesn't mean a low entry price that masks expensive yearly increases. We need a vendor whose pricing is transparent and bundled to avoid those creeping add-on costs.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Mizuki, transparency in pricing is useful, but it is secondary to operational control. A bundled price is meaningless if the system's scope drifts and we are forced to pay for constant, unplanned modifications just to keep it functional.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,3,talk,Rin,,"@Asuka, but if the system is too hard to change, it just becomes a sleepy dinosaur like the dispatch tool! We need something that stays fresh and doesn't need a million ""fixes"" from outside people who don't care!","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,3,talk,Servas,,Wisdom teaches us that the most sophisticated tool is useless if the hand that holds it is exhausted. We must prioritize a transition that is gentle on our remaining experts and intuitive for our crews.,"{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
55,2,4,talk,Kenji,,"I'm with you, Servas! We can't let the people get exhausted. Let's find a vendor that's actually tough and simple enough that the depot crews will love it and not feel stressed!","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
56,2,4,talk,Mizuki,,"I agree, Kenji. However, we must be vigilant. A system that is ""simple"" to use but complex to pay for will eventually create a different kind of stress for the organization. Let us seek a partner that is honest about the total cost of ownership.","{""words"":45,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
57,2,4,talk,Asuka,,"@Mizuki, honesty about cost is a baseline, not a solution. We must prioritize a vendor that provides a single point of accountability and a flexible framework for scope changes. Otherwise, we are just buying a more expensive version of our current failure.","{""words"":42,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
58,2,4,talk,Rin,,I agree! We need a partner who actually stays to help us and doesn't just leave a mess behind. Let's pick someone who keeps things fresh and doesn't let our tools turn into dinosaurs! I'm ready for the candidates!,"{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
59,2,4,talk,Servas,,"It is a comfort to see us all aligning our hearts toward the well-being of the company. Let us carry this mindfulness into the final selection, ensuring we choose a path that honors both our budget and our people.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
